\splytwnine\ftnlytwnine\htmautsp\nolnhtadjtbl\useltbaln\alntblind\lytcalctblwd\lyttblrtgr\lnbrkrule\nobrkwrptbl\snaptogridincell\allowfieldendsel\ApplyBrkRules\wrppunct\asianbrkrule\rsidroot6310308\newtblstyruls\nogrowautofit\paperh16840\paperw11907\margl744\margr5407\margb360\margt465\pard\li2226\ri-3\qr\fi0\sl-398\f00 \fs25{\fs25\charscalex111\b\i0\ul0\f00 Notes Toward A Reading of Foscolo's "Sepolcri" \par}
\pard
\pard\li4108\ri26\sb254\ql\fi0\sl-230\f00 \fs16{\fs16\charscalex114\b0\i0\ul0\f00 P. \fs22\charscalex106\f10 M. \fs16\charscalex114\f00 PASINETTI \par}
\pard
\pard\li9\ri25\sb528\qj\fi475\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 Among non-Italians, or, for that matter, among non\f02 \-\f00 literary Italians who know about it from hearsay or from dim scholastic recollection, a certa in type of Italian poetry, roughly from Alfieri to Carducci, is assumed to possess mostly local patriotic intere st. This is the poetry in which the theme of the Nation is more or less clearly centraI, and the poet, in one way or another, takes the position of national \fs21\charscalex100\i vates. \fs21\charscalex106\i0 He contributes to creating the poetic myth of his Nation. In that sense he poses himself as a recorder and also as a prophet, in his evocation of past glories, which it is his function to immortalize through poetry, and in his vision of a future more glorious than the presento Vittorio Alfieri in the sonnet \fs21\charscalex100\i Giorno verrà \fs21\charscalex106\i0 explicitly visu\f02 \-\f00 alized his countrymen's future recognition of his prophecies: \par}
\pard
\pard\li720\ri754\sb81\ql\fi0\sl-244\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 Gli odo già dirmi: - O Vate nostro, in pravi Secoli nato, eppur create hai queste Sublimi età, che profetando andavi. \par}
\pard
\pard\ri39\sb81\qj\fi475\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 The theme of past glory, depraved present, and future hope of resurgence, has, to mention the obvious, a long tradition in Italian literature from Dante through Petrarch to the last chapter of \fs21\charscalex100\i The Prince. \fs21\charscalex106\i0 Later on, it may seem particularly natura l to tum to nineteenth-century ltaly for examples of "patriotic Romanticism." However, once we do so, it is significant that while we may have justified the direction of our choice on historical considerations, we end by finding ourselves on literary grounds; we have, in other words, an image of the Nation based, to an overwhelmingly large extent, on literature. That image has a long exis\f02 \-\f00 tence in poetic texts before it acquires one in political reality \par}
\pard
\pard\li5572\ri25\ql\fi0\sl-340\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex135\b0\i0\ul0\f00 3 \par}
\pard
\sbknone\sect\pard\sectd\paperh16840\paperw11907\margl1065\margr4967\margb360\margt796\pard\ri105\sb4\ql\fi0\sl-244\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 and oratory; in fact, the poetic image patently inspires and pervades the political idea. \par}
\pard
\pard\li33\ri28\sb19\qj\fi475\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 A poet' s vision of his \fs20\charscalex100\i patria, \fs21\charscalex106\i0 unless we mistake the texts of most national anthems for poetry, can be a very complex one. The way in which the \fs20\charscalex100\i vates \fs21\charscalex106\i0 handles the themes of allegiance, of communal sorrow, of glorious evo· cation, of exhortation, of prophecy, can among other things provide subtle insights into the very idea of the Nation as a cultura l entity and a symbol of the human condition in generaI. Something like the notion \fs24\charscalex82\i oI \fs21\charscalex106\i0 the "concrete uni\f02 \-\f00 versaI" in poetry may once morebe he1pful: the poet is not simply a sort of officiating celebratòr, but while still partly maintaining that position he also fumishes a more concrete suggestion of individuality and \fs24\charscalex82\i oI \fs21\charscalex106\i0 individuaI drama, and a more immediately human reference to a cultural tradition very personally experienced, indeed even to a so\f02 \-\f00 ci al and episodical context. \fs23\charscalex87\b It \fs21\charscalex106\b0 is extremely difficult to make this kind of poetry a successo The uniquely successful ex\f02 \-\f00 ampIe I ha ve in mind is F oscolo' s \fs20\charscalex100\i Dei sepolcri \fs21\charscalex106\i0 (1807), a very compact poem of 295 unrhymed eleven-syllable lines, to Italians one of the most famous in the language and generally one of the most difficult. \par}
\pard
\pard\li33\ri28\sb19\qj\fi475\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 How is a balance achieved here between the official and the personal, the public and the intimate? The poet's voice is, t6 a considerable extent, one \fs23\charscalex84\i oI \fs21\charscalex106\i0 eloquent com\f02 \-\f00 memoration and exhortation; but at the same time we see that the poem exists also as a different kind of expression -as the personal musing, as the familiar address to a friend. If we should force it into a formula, we might say that the poem introduces the manner of the poetic epistle into a tissue largely woven out of "Romantic" materials and that the most prominent among these are in the traditions of Night Poetry, of Cultural Patriotism, and of Romantic Hellenism. \par}
\pard
\pard\li105\sb48\qj\fi460\sl-235\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 The main scene and the principal sources of the imagery are the burial ground, the sepulcher, the urn, the emblemlS in which the living preserve and honor the memory of the dead. The commemorative function of those objects is, in the end, identified with that of the poet as \fs20\charscalex100\i vates, \fs21\charscalex106\i0 for the \par}
\pard
\pard\li120\ql\fi0\sl-321\f00 \fs20{\fs20\charscalex125\b0\i0\ul0\f00 4 \par}
\pard
\sbkpage\sect\pard\sectd\paperh16840\paperw11907\margl840\margr5259\margb360\margt508\pard\ri24\sb14\qj\fi0\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex105\b0\i0\ul0\f00 poem is not only in the \fs22\charscalex100\i vates \fs21\charscalex105\i0 tradition, it is also \fs22\charscalex100\i about \fs21\charscalex105\i0 it: poetry establishes, and preserves for posterity, the memory of the great. The conventions of graveyard poetry are thus associated with t.~e time-honored concept of the poet as preserver and solemnizer of the memories of heroes. It is not particularly frivolous to say that the poem suggests the image of a classical urn on a background of Romantic darkness. The image in which the centraI theme of the poem is most concisely stated (" ... le Muse .. Siedon custodi de' sepolcri,") suggests almost inevitably a neo\f02 \-\f00 classic marble group, in so far as poetry can ever use\f02 \-\f00 fully recall sculpture. This aspect of the poem seems to justify the passages of high and solemn eloquence, the elab\f02 \-\f00 orate constructions, the Latinisms. All this, however, is constantly interwoven with other aspects and tones -that of personal meditation, that of the familiar address, and finally that of concretely "civic" poetry, \fs22\charscalex100\i poesia civile. \fs21\charscalex105\i0 The image of the poet himself corresponds to an image of the Nation both as idealized myth and as individuaI ex\f02 \-\f00 perience in a defined historical and social setting. The shifts are worked out in various ways, of which a brief survey of the poem may give some idea. \par}
\pard
\pard\li24\ri15\sb76\qj\fi475\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex105\b0\i0\ul0\f00 The most immediate and localized elements are \fs17\charscalex115\i\f10 pro\f12 \-\fs21\charscalex105\i0\f02 \f00 vided by the event which has given the poem its practi\f02 \-\f00 cal occasiono A decree, intended to pIace cemeteries out of the cities and to control the custom of inscriptions on graves, has been taken by the poet to signify decay of com\f02 \-\f00 munal reverence toward the dead and of ritual piety. In so far as he claims that motif as his startingpoint (it be\f02 \-\f00 comes rather secondary in the actual working of the poem) the poet assumes his first rale, indicating that he possesses certa in practical "civic" connections and interests and singling himself out as the defender of higher values against the present rulers of his society. In fact the poem opens when that "occasion" has already given precedence to the more generaI meditation on death, and on the value of the "illusion" of graves. "confortate di pianto." But the opening has also the function of preparing for a more ef\f02 \-\f00 fective introduction of the "occasionaI" argument. \par}
\pard
\pard\li5677\ri1\sb52\ql\fi0\sl-230\f00 \fs22{\fs22\charscalex131\b0\i0\ul0\f00 5 \par}
\pard
\sbkpage\sect\pard\sectd\paperh16840\paperw11907\margl854\margr5178\margb360\margt374\pard\ri96\qj\fi475\sl-244\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex107\b0\i0\ul0\f00 The initial points of the poem are largely made through questions, Pàrtly rhetorical, aligned almost as variations and amplifications on the theme proposed bythe first one -proposed, one would almost say, scholastically, as though to be debated and elaborated on by what will follow, in logical progression: \par}
\pard
\pard\li734\ri1363\sb110\qj\fi0\sl-244\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex107\b0\i0\ul0\f00 All'ombra dei cipressi e dentro l'ume Confortate di pianto \fs21\charscalex100 è \fs21\charscalex107 forse il sonno Della morte men duro? \par}
\pard
\pard\li48\ri52\sb115\qj\fi465\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex107\b0\i0\ul0\f00 There is the tone of solemn meditation on death, but it goes along with that of the familiar poetic musing ad\f02 \-\f00 dressed to a friend - a fellow-poet, Ippolito Pindemonte. So the questions acquire a conversational quality, the ad\f02 \-\f00 dress is almost an exchange: \par}
\pard
\pard\li768\ri1238\sb115\ql\fi0\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex107\b0\i0\ul0\f00 Qual fia ristoro a' dì perduti un sasso Che distingua le mie dalle infinite \par}
