\pard\li768\ri974\ql\fi0\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex107\b0\i0\ul0\f00 Ossa che in terra e in mal' semina morte? Vero \fs20\charscalex100 è \fs21\charscalex107 ben, Pindemonte ... \par}
\pard
\pard\li48\ri52\sb115\qj\fi465\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex107\b0\i0\ul0\f00 The tone of the relationship existing between the two friends ("Né da te, dolce amico, udrò piu il verso / E la mesta armonia che lo govema ... ") aptly prepares us to the nature of the point which is finally made -the persistence of similar communions in friendship after death -a point which is developed in the lofty passage con\f02 \-\f00 cluding what we may consider the first section of the poem (to line 50). \par}
\pard
\pard\li81\sb38\qj\fi465\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex107\b0\i0\ul0\f00 A second section can be singled out, comprising lines 51-151. After the generaI argumentative introduction has established the theme of the "corrispondenza" between the dead and the living as the humans' "celeste dote," the oration, as it were, starts on its specific business. The "new law" on cemeteries is briefly mentioned at the start; then with an immediate transition, which is typical of the rather "telescoped" quality of the whole work, the un\f02 \-\f00 lamented grave of the poet Giuseppe Parini is given as instance of present abandonment and oblivion. It also oc\f02 \-\f00 casions a passage in the moonlight-horror variety of Ro- \par}
\pard
\pard\li124\ql\fi0\sl-336\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex121\b0\i0\ul0\f00 6 \par}
