\pard\li48\sb38\qj\fi480\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 Firmly placed in the present tense is only the vision of the ruling society against which the poet addresses his most polemical passage. This is at the end of the second section (137-150): the ròle he assumes here is that of the poet-citizen, deprecating the false values of a governing class whose "ministri" are "l'opulenza e il tremore" and whose cult of the dead is cold ostentation. Re sets him- \par}
