\pard\sl-1\ls2\par\pard\li9\ri39\sb321\qj\fi0\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 self against that vain pomp and deceitful stability; in con\f02 \-\f00 trast to them, throughout the poem - much in the tradition of Rbmantic patriotism where the pattern of exile is typical - he describes himself as "fuggitivo," his life as "raminga," so that the prospect of his own death and burial is given on some such implicit premise as "if ever Fortune grant me any repose;" and, explicitly, his only legacy to his fellow\f02 \-\f00 souls is not going to be vain treasures but passionately and freely conceived poetry ("Non di tesori eredità, ma caldi / Sensi e di liberaI carme l'esempio.") \par}
