\pard\li9\ri39\qj\fi0\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 now that theme reappears in a more solemn and public connection, the stress being on the example and the message left after death. \par}
\pard
\pard\li33\ri10\sb57\qj\fi465\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 The transition from this point is naturally to the sec\f02 \-\f00 tion (151-197) where the poet assumes the function of the communal celebrator of heroes and evokes the memories \fs24\charscalex100\i oi \fs21\charscalex106\i0 some of the country's great men of the past through the vision of their tombs in the church of the Holy Cross, in Florence; the fact that he proposes their examples immedi\f02 \-\f00 ately after having looked forward to establishing his own, is, of course, significant. Rhetorically, the fact that the great dead (Machiavelli, Michelangelo, Galileo) are not named but indicated through eleborate though concise cir\f02 \-\f00 cumlocution (a method not infrequent in Dante, in fact used here by Foscolo al so in his allusion to Dante himself and to Petrarch) may represent the kind of altifice con\f02 \-\f00 sidered appropriate to the high occasion, but may also sug\f02 \-\f00 gest the convention of the \charscalex100\i vates \charscalex106\i0 whose message is veiled in riddle. \par}
\pard
\pard\li33\ri10\sb57\qj\fi465\sl-240\f00 \fs21{\fs21\charscalex106\b0\i0\ul0\f00 The experience, however, is made familiar by an in\f02 \-\f00 tense awareness of the scene, particularly the characteriza\f02 \-\f00 tion of the landscape surrounding the city, the moonlit hills, the "convalli / Popolate di case e d'oliveti." The vision is limited in space though not in ti me ; this is a passage of "civic" poetry al so in the sense that it direcÙY cele- \par}
