\sbkpage\sect\pard\sectd\paperh16840\paperw11907\margl624\margr5442\margb360\margt456\pard\ri67\sb4\qj\fi0\sl-240\f00 \fs20{\fs20\charscalex105\b0\i0\ul0\f00 and mytholQgical material cannot be analyzed in detail here; but I shalljtry to mention some of the ways in which the generaI unity of tone of the whole poem is achieved and to see how the image of the \fs21\charscalex100\i vates \fs20\charscalex105\i0 is established in various guises and provides the dominating point of view. \fs25\charscalex88\f10 e') \par}
\pard
\pard\li9\ri43\sb9\qj\fi475\sl-235\f00 \fs20{\fs20\charscalex105\b0\i0\ul0\f00 The shift in time had been obviously prepared by many previous allusions; for instance, in the "didactic" section of the poem we saw archaic elements used not as decora\f02 \-\f00 tive reference but as present, operating forces. The poet, as it wel'e, lived not only by pl'esent-day codes but by ancient ones as well, as he alluded to Rope "ultima dea," to "templi acherontei," to "aul'a de' beati Elisi," etc. Along that line, we have now a sort of formaI adoption of the l'aIe of the officiating \fs21\charscalex100\i vates: \par}
\pard
